  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 120,
    "dDurationMs": 2680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What will be at stake will \nnot just be cool products "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2800,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But whether liberal democracy survives,\nWhether the CCP survives, what the world  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 6880,
    "dDurationMs": 3200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "order for the next century is going to be\nThe CCP is going to have an all out  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 10080,
    "dDurationMs": 3400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "effort to infiltrate American AI labs\nBillions of dollars, thousands of people "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 13480,
    "dDurationMs": 2800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "CCP is going to try to out-build us.\nPeople don’t realize like how  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 16280,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "intense state level espionage can be\nWhen we have like literal superintelligence "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 20160,
    "dDurationMs": 2800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They can like Stuxnet the chinese data centers\nYou really think that will be like  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 22960,
    "dDurationMs": 1560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a private company\nAnd the government  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 24520,
    "dDurationMs": 4040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "wouldn’t be like “oh my god what is going on?”\nI do think it is incredibly important that these  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 28560,
    "dDurationMs": 2360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "clusters are in the united states\nI mean would you do the manhattan  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 30920,
    "dDurationMs": 3040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "project in the UAE?\n2023 was the moment for me  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 33960,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "when it went from AGI as this sort of theoretical, \nabstract thing, and you’d make the models to like,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 38160,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I see it, I feel it. I can see the cluster where \nit’s trained on, like the rough combination of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 41840,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "algorithms, the people, like how it’s happening, \nand I think most of the world is not; most of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 46120,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the people who feel it are like right here\nToday I’m chatting with my friend Leopold  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 51640,
    "dDurationMs": 6680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Aschenbrenner. He grew up in Germany and graduated \nas valedictorian of Columbia when he was 19. After  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 58320,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that, he had a very interesting gap year which \nwe’ll talk about. Then, he was on the OpenAI  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 64280,
    "dDurationMs": 6680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "superalignment team, may it rest in peace.\nNow, with some anchor investments — from  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 70960,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Patrick and John Collison, Daniel Gross, and Nat \nFriedman — he is launching an investment firm. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 77000,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Leopold, you’re off to a slow start but \nlife is long. I wouldn’t worry about it  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 81560,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "too much. You’ll make up for it in due \ntime. Thanks for coming on the podcast. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 86800,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you. I first discovered your \npodcast when your best episode had  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 91360,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a couple of hundred views. It’s been amazing to \nfollow your trajectory. It’s a delight to be on. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 98880,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the Sholto and Trenton episode, I mentioned \nthat a lot of the things I’ve learned about AI  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 104000,
    "dDurationMs": 3000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I’ve learned from talking with them. The \nthird, and probably most significant, part  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 107000,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of this triumvirate has been you. We’ll \nget all the stuff on the record now. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 113600,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Here’s the first thing I want to get on the \nrecord. Tell me about the trillion-dollar cluster. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 119240,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I should mention this for the context of \nthe podcast. Today you’re releasing a series  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 124160,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "called Situational Awareness. We’re going to \nget into it. First question about that is,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 127880,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tell me about the trillion-dollar cluster.\nUnlike most things that have recently come  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 133120,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "out of Silicon Valley, AI is an industrial \nprocess. The next model doesn’t just require  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 139920,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "some code. It’s building a giant new cluster. \nIt’s building giant new power plants. Pretty soon,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 145240,
    "dDurationMs": 7160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it’s going to involve building giant new fabs.\nSince ChatGPT, this extraordinary techno-capital  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 152400,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "acceleration has been set into \nmotion. Exactly a year ago today,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 157080,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Nvidia had their first blockbuster earnings call. \nIt went up 25% after hours and everyone was like,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 162000,
    "dDurationMs": 8080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "\"oh my God, AI is a thing.\" Within a year, Nvidia \ndata center revenue has gone from a few billion a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 170080,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quarter to $25 billion a quarter and continues \nto go up. Big Tech capex is skyrocketing. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 179320,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s funny. There’s this crazy scramble going on, \nbut in some sense it’s just the continuation of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 184800,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "straight lines on a graph. There’s this long-run \ntrend of almost a decade of training compute  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 189520,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for the largest AI systems growing by about \nhalf an order of magnitude, 0.5 OOMs a year. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 196400,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Just play that forward. GPT-4 was reported \nto have finished pre-training in 2022.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 203360,
    "dDurationMs": 10600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On SemiAnalysis, it was rumored to have a cluster \nsize of about 25,000 A100s. That’s roughly a $500  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 213960,
    "dDurationMs": 9120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "million cluster. Very roughly, it’s 10 megawatts.\nJust play that forward half a year. By 2024,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 223880,
    "dDurationMs": 9000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that’s a cluster that’s 100 MW and 100,000 \nH100 equivalents with costs in the billions. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 232880,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Play it forward two more years. By 2026, that’s \na gigawatt, the size of a large nuclear reactor.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 239400,
    "dDurationMs": 3200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s like the power of the Hoover Dam. \nThat costs tens of billions of dollars  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 242600,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and requires a million H100 equivalents.\nBy 2028, that’s a cluster that’s ten GW.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 246880,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s more power than most US states. \nThat’s 10 million H100 equivalents,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 252240,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "costing hundreds of billions of dollars.\nBy 2030, you get the trillion-dollar cluster  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 258280,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "using 100 gigawatts, over 20% of US electricity \nproduction. That’s 100 million H100 equivalents. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 265240,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s just the training cluster. There are more \ninference GPUs as well. Once there are products,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 270760,
    "dDurationMs": 8520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "most of them will be inference GPUs. US power \nproduction has barely grown for decades.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 279280,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now we’re really in for a ride.\nWhen I had Zuck on the podcast,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 283840,
    "dDurationMs": 7560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "he was claiming not a plateau per se, but that AI \nprogress would be bottlenecked by this constraint  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 291400,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on energy. Specifically, he was like, \"oh, \ngigawatt data centers, are we going to build  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 295840,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "another Three Gorges Dam or something?\"\nAccording to public reports, there  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 301440,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are companies planning things on the scale of \na 1 GW data center. With a 10 GW data center,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 306560,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "who’s going to be able to build that? A 100 GW \ncenter is like a state project. Are you going  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 311520,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to pump that into one physical data center? How \nis it going to be possible? What is Zuck missing? "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 318800,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Six months ago, 10 GW was the talk of the town. \nNow, people have moved on. 10 GW is happening.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 325400,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There’s The Information report on OpenAI and \nMicrosoft planning a $100 billion cluster. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 331720,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Is that 1 GW? Or is that 10 GW?\nI don’t know but if you try to map  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 335640,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "out how expensive the 10 GW cluster would be, \nthat’s a couple of hundred billion. It’s sort  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 339960,
    "dDurationMs": 10360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of on that scale and they’re planning it. \nIt’s not just my crazy take. AMD forecasted  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 350320,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a $400 billion AI accelerator market by 2027. AI \naccelerators are only part of the expenditures. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 356880,
    "dDurationMs": 7400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’re very much on track for a $1 trillion of \ntotal AI investment by 2027. The $1 trillion  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 364280,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cluster will take a bit more acceleration. We \nsaw how much ChatGPT unleashed. Every generation,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 371640,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the models are going to be crazy \nand shift the Overton window. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 375720,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Then the revenue comes in. These are \nforward-looking investments. The question is,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 379320,
    "dDurationMs": 8120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "do they pay off? Let’s estimate the GPT-4 cluster \nat around $500 million. There’s a common mistake  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 387440,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "people make, saying it was $100 million for GPT-4. \nThat’s just the rental price. If you’re building  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 394640,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the biggest cluster, you have to build and pay \nfor the whole cluster. You can’t just rent it  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 398400,
    "dDurationMs": 2080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for three months.\nCan’t you? "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 400480,
    "dDurationMs": 2480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Once you’re trying to get into the \nhundreds of billions, you have to get  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 402960,
    "dDurationMs": 2840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to like $100 billion a year in revenue. This \nis where it gets really interesting for the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 405800,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "big tech companies because their revenues \nare on the order of hundreds of billions. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 410280,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "$10 billion is fine. It’ll pay off \nthe 2024 size training cluster.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 416960,
    "dDurationMs": 2880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’ll really be gangbusters with Big Tech when \nit costs $100 billion a year. The question is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 419840,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "how feasible is $100 billion a year from \nAI revenue? It’s a lot more than right  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 425240,
    "dDurationMs": 7560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "now. If you believe in the trajectory of \nAI systems as I do, it’s not that crazy. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 432800,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are like 300 million Microsoft Office \nsubscribers. They have Copilot now. I don’t  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 439040,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "know what they’re selling it for. Suppose \nyou sold some AI add-on for $100/month to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 445480,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a third of Microsoft Office subscribers. That’d \nbe $100 billion right there. $100/month is a lot. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 450680,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s a lot for a third of Office subscribers.\nFor the average knowledge worker, it’s a few  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 455720,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hours of productivity a month. You have \nto be expecting pretty lame AI progress to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 460160,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not hit a few hours of productivity a month.\nSure, let’s assume all this. What happens in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 467520,
    "dDurationMs": 8600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the next few years? What can the AI trained \non the 1 GW data center do? What about the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 476120,
    "dDurationMs": 3320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one on the 10 GW data center? Just map out \nthe next few years of AI progress for me. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 481080,
    "dDurationMs": 7320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The 10 GW range is my best guess \nfor when you get true AGI. Compute  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 488400,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is actually overrated. We’ll talk about that.\nBy 2025-2026, we’re going to get models that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 494200,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are basically smarter than most college \ngraduates. A lot of the economic usefulness  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 501400,
    "dDurationMs": 7720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "depends on unhobbling. The models are smart \nbut limited. There are chatbots and then there  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 509800,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are things like being able to use a computer \nand doing agentic long-horizon tasks. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 515320,
    "dDurationMs": 11120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "By 2027-2028, it’ll get as smart as the smartest \nexperts. The unhobbling trajectory points to it  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 526440,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "becoming much more like an agent than a chatbot. \nIt’ll almost be like a drop-in remote worker. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 533680,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is the question around the economic returns. \nIntermediate AI systems could be really useful,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 539000,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but it takes a lot of schlep to \nintegrate them. There’s a lot you  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 544640,
    "dDurationMs": 2840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "could do with GPT-4 or GPT-4.5 in a business \nuse case, but you really have to change your  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 547480,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "workflows to make them useful. It’s a very Tyler \nCowen-esque take. It just takes a long time to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 552640,
    "dDurationMs": 9000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "diffuse. We’re in SF and so we miss that.\nBut in some sense, the way these systems  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 561640,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "want to be integrated is where you get this kind \nof sonic boom. Intermediate systems could have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 568280,
    "dDurationMs": 3160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "done it, but it would have taken schlep. Before \nyou do the schlep to integrate them, you’ll get  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 572920,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "much more powerful systems that are unhobbled.\nThey’re agents, drop-in remote workers. You’re  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 579160,
    "dDurationMs": 3432,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "interacting with them like coworkers. You \ncan do Zoom calls and Slack with them. You  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 584800,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can ask them to do a project and they go off and \nwrite a first draft, get feedback, run tests on  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 591640,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their code, and come back. Then you can tell them \nmore things. That’ll be much easier to integrate. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 600120,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You might need a bit of overkill to make \nthe transition easy and harvest the gains. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 605360,
    "dDurationMs": 2760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What do you mean by overkill? \nOverkill on model capabilities? "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 608120,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Yeah, the intermediate models could do it but \nit would take a lot of schlep. The drop-in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 613200,
    "dDurationMs": 7240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "remote worker AGI can automate cognitive \ntasks. The intermediate models would have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 620440,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "made the software engineer more productive. \nBut will the software engineer adopt it? "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 624120,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "With the 2027 model, you just don’t need the \nsoftware engineer. You can interact with it  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 628920,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like a software engineer, and it’ll \ndo the work of a software engineer. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 632280,
    "dDurationMs": 8360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The last episode I did was with John Schulman.\nI was asking about this. We have these models  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 640640,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that have come out in the last year and none seem \nto have significantly surpassed GPT-4, certainly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 646240,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not in an agentic way where they interact \nwith you as a coworker. They’ll brag about  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 651800,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a few extra points on MMLU. Even with GPT-4o, \nit’s cool they can talk like Scarlett Johansson  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 663320,
    "dDurationMs": 8560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "(I guess not anymore) but \nit’s not like a coworker. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 673760,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It makes sense why they’d be good at answering \nquestions. They have data on how to complete  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 678880,
    "dDurationMs": 8320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Wikipedia text. Where is the equivalent training \ndata to understand a Zoom call? Referring back to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 687200,
    "dDurationMs": 3640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "your point about a Slack conversation, \nhow can it use context to figure out the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 690840,
    "dDurationMs": 7000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cohesive project you’re working on? \nWhere is that training data coming from? "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 698560,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A key question for AI progress in the next few \nyears is how hard it is to unlock the test time  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 704880,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "compute overhang. Right now, GPT-4 can do a few \nhundred tokens with chain-of-thought. That’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 712320,
    "dDurationMs": 7920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "already a huge improvement. Before, answering a \nmath question was just shotgun. If you tried to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 720240,
    "dDurationMs": 3240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "answer a math question by saying the first thing \nthat comes to mind, you wouldn’t be very good. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 723480,
    "dDurationMs": 14280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "GPT-4 thinks for a few hundred tokens. If \nI think at 100 tokens a minute, that’s like  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 737760,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "what GPT-4 does. It’s equivalent to me thinking \nfor three minutes. Suppose GPT-4 could think  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 744320,
    "dDurationMs": 7560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for millions of tokens. That’s +4 OOMs on test \ntime compute on one problem. It can’t do it now.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 751880,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It gets stuck. It writes some code. It can do a \nlittle bit of iterative debugging, but eventually  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 757560,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gets stuck and can’t correct its errors.\nThere’s a big overhang. In other areas of ML,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 765320,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there’s a great paper on AlphaGo, where you can \ntrade off train time and test time compute. If  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 769800,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you can use 4 OOMs more test time compute, \nthat’s almost like a 3.5x OOM bigger model. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 774880,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Again, if it’s 100 tokens a minute, a few million \ntokens is a few months of working time. There’s a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 780880,
    "dDurationMs": 2800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lot more you can do in a few months of working \ntime than just getting an answer right now. The  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 783680,
    "dDurationMs": 7640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "question is how hard is it to unlock that?\nIn the short timelines AI world,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 791320,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it’s not that hard. The reason it might not be \nthat hard is that there are only a few extra  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 796920,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tokens to learn. You need to learn things like \nerror correction tokens where you’re like “ah,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 802280,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I made a mistake, let me think about that again.” \nYou need to learn planning tokens where it’s like  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 805800,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "“I’m going to start by making a plan. Here’s my \nplan of attack. I’m going to write a draft and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 810520,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "now I’m going to critique my draft and think about \nit.” These aren’t things that models can do now,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 815360,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but the question is how hard it is.\nThere are two paths to agents. When  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 821960,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Sholto was on your podcast, he talked about \nscaling leading to more nines of reliability.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 828000,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s one path. The other path is the \nunhobbling path. It needs to learn this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 833240,
    "dDurationMs": 9040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "System 2 process. If it can learn that, it can \nuse millions of tokens and think coherently. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 844760,
    "dDurationMs": 9200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Here’s an analogy. When you drive, you’re \non autopilot most of the time. Sometimes you  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 853960,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hit a weird construction zone or intersection. \nSometimes my girlfriend is in the passenger seat  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 858840,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and I’m like “ah, be quiet for a moment, \nI need to figure out what’s going on.” "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 863720,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You go from autopilot to System 2 and \nyou’re thinking about how to do it. Scaling  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 869200,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "improves that System 1 autopilot. The brute force \nway to get to agents is improving that system. If  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 873840,
    "dDurationMs": 10000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you can get System 2 working, you can \nquickly jump to something more agentified  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 883840,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and test time compute overhang is unlocked.\nWhat’s the reason to think this is an easy  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 889920,
    "dDurationMs": 10600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "win? Is there some loss function that easily \nenables System 2 thinking? There aren’t many  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 900520,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "animals with System 2 thinking. It took a long \ntime for evolution to give us System 2 thinking. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 905440,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Pre-training has trillions of tokens of Internet \ntext, I get that. You match that and get  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 911800,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all of these free training capabilities. What’s \nthe reason to think this is an easy unhobbling? "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 922000,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "First of all, pre-training is magical. \nIt gave us a huge advantage for models of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 929360,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "general intelligence because you can predict the \nnext token. But there’s a common misconception.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 935680,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Predicting the next token lets the model learn \nincredibly rich representations. Representation  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 939760,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "learning properties are the magic of \ndeep learning. Rather than just learning  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 944720,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "statistical artifacts, the models learn models \nof the world. That’s why they can generalize,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 949560,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because it learned the right representations.\nWhen you train a model, you have this raw bundle  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 955720,
    "dDurationMs": 10840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of capabilities that’s useful. The unhobbling \nfrom GPT-2 to GPT-4 took this raw mass and RLHF’d  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 966560,
    "dDurationMs": 6920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it into a good chatbot. That was a huge win.\nIn the original InstructGPT paper, comparing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 973480,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "RLHF vs. non-RLHF models it’s like a 100x model \nsize win on human preference rating. It started  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 978800,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to be able to do simple chain-of-thought and \nso on. But you still have this advantage of all  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 983040,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "these raw capabilities, and there’s still \na huge amount you’re not doing with them. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 988960,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This pre-training advantage is also the difference \nto robotics. People used to say it was a hardware  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 995920,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "problem. The hardware is getting solved, \nbut you don’t have this huge advantage  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1001080,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of bootstrapping with pre-training. You don’t \nhave all this unsupervised learning you can do.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1005320,
    "dDurationMs": 11160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You have to start right away with RL self-play.\nThe question is why RL and unhobbling might work.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1020040,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Bootstrapping is an advantage. Your Twitter \nbio is being pre-trained. You’re not being  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1025840,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pre-trained anymore. You were pre-trained in \ngrade school and high school. At some point,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1030000,
    "dDurationMs": 7000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you transition to being able to learn by yourself. \nYou weren’t able to do it in elementary school.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1038760,
    "dDurationMs": 6920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "High school is probably where it started and by \ncollege, if you’re smart, you can teach yourself.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1046200,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Models are just starting to enter that regime.\nIt’s a little bit more scaling and then you figure  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1052560,
    "dDurationMs": 8760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "out what goes on top. It won’t be trivial. A lot \nof deep learning seems obvious in retrospect.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1061320,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There’s some obvious cluster of ideas. There \nare some ideas that seem a little dumb but  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1066920,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "work. There are a lot of details you have to \nget right. We’re not going to get this next  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1070760,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "month. It’ll take a while to figure out.\nA while for you is like half a year. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1075920,
    "dDurationMs": 10400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I don’t know, between six months and three \nyears. But it's possible. It’s also very  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1086320,
    "dDurationMs": 9040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "related to the issue of the data wall. Here’s one \nintuition on learning by yourself. Pre-training  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1095360,
    "dDurationMs": 7653,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is kind of like the teacher lecturing to \nyou and the words are flying by. You’re  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1104040,
    "dDurationMs": 4120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just getting a little bit from it.\nThat's not what you do when you learn  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1108160,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by yourself. When you learn by yourself, \nsay you're reading a dense math textbook,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1112360,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you're not just skimming through it once. \nSome wordcels just skim through and reread  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1117600,
    "dDurationMs": 7640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and reread the math textbook and they memorize.\nWhat you do is you read a page, think about it,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1125240,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have some internal monologue going on, and have \na conversation with a study buddy. You try a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1129320,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "practice problem and fail a bunch of times. \nAt some point it clicks, and you're like,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1133400,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "\"this made sense.\" Then you read a few more pages.\nWe've kind of bootstrapped our way to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1139800,
    "dDurationMs": 8920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just starting to be able to do that \nnow with models. The question is,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1148720,
    "dDurationMs": 9840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can you use all this sort of self-play, synthetic \ndata, RL to make that thing work. Right now,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1158560,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there's in-context learning, which is super \nsample efficient. In the Gemini paper, it just  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1163320,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "learns a language in-context. Pre-training, on \nthe other hand, is not at all sample efficient. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1170920,
    "dDurationMs": 3200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What humans do is a kind of in-context \nlearning. You read a book, think about it,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1174120,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "until eventually it clicks. Then you somehow \ndistill that back into the weights. In some sense,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1179560,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that's what RL is trying to do. RL is super \nfinicky, but when it works it's kind of magical. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1186320,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It's the best possible data for the model. \nIt’s when you try a practice problem, fail,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1191920,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and at some point figure it out in a way \nthat makes sense to you. That's the best  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1196280,
    "dDurationMs": 2360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "possible data for you because it's the \nway you would have solved the problem,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1202000,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rather than just reading how somebody else solved \nthe problem, which doesn't initially click. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1207160,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "By the way, if that take sounds familiar it's \nbecause it was part of the question I asked  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1210600,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "John Schulman. It goes to illustrate the thing \nI said in the intro. A bunch of the things I've  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1215200,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "learned about AI comes from these dinners we \ndo before the interviews with me, you, Sholto,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1220320,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and a couple of others. We’re like, “what should \nI ask John Schulman, what I should ask Dario.” "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1226200,
    "dDurationMs": 3560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Suppose this is the way things \ngo and we get these unhobblings— "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1231000,
    "dDurationMs": 7920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And the scaling. You have this baseline of this \nenormous force of scaling. GPT-2 was amazing. It  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1238920,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "could string together plausible sentences, but it \ncould barely do anything. It was kind of like a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1243480,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "preschooler. GPT-4, on the other hand, could write \ncode and do hard math, like a smart high schooler.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1249600,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This big jump in capability is explored in the \nessay series. I count the orders of magnitude  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1253120,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of compute and scale-up of algorithmic progress.\nScaling alone by 2027-2028 is going to do another  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1260240,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "preschool to high school jump on top of GPT-4. At \na per token level, the models will be incredibly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1267440,
    "dDurationMs": 4040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "smart. They'll gain more reliability, and with the \naddition of unhobblings, they'll look less like  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1271480,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "chatbots and more like agents or drop-in remote \nworkers. That's when things really get going. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1280280,
    "dDurationMs": 7880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I want to ask more questions about this but let's \nzoom out. Suppose you're right about this. This is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1288160,
    "dDurationMs": 9440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because of the 2027 cluster which is at 10 GW?\n2028 is 10 GW. Maybe it'll be pulled forward. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1297600,
    "dDurationMs": 7720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Something like a 5.5 level by 2027, whatever \nthat's called. What does the world look like at  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1305320,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that point? You have these remote workers who can \nreplace people. What is the reaction to that in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1311200,
    "dDurationMs": 9720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "terms of the economy, politics, and geopolitics?\n2023 was a really interesting year to experience  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1320920,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as somebody who was really following the AI stuff.\nWhat were you doing in 2023? "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1326840,
    "dDurationMs": 13280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OpenAI. When you were at OpenAI in 2023, it was a \nweird thing. You almost didn't want to talk about  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1340120,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "AI or AGI. It was kind of a dirty word. Then \nin 2023, people saw ChatGPT for the first time,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1345160,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they saw GPT-4, and it just exploded.\nIt triggered huge capital expenditures  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1351560,
    "dDurationMs": 7880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from all these firms and an explosion in revenue \nfrom Nvidia and so on. Things have been quiet  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1359440,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "since then, but the next thing has been in the \noven. I expect every generation these g-forces  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1364280,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to intensify. People will see the models. \nThey won’t have counted the OOMs so they're  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1370360,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "going to be surprised. It'll be kind of crazy.\nRevenue is going to accelerate. Suppose you  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1374600,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "do hit $10 billion by the end of this year. \nSuppose it just continues on the trajectory  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1378920,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of revenue doubling every six months. It's not \nactually that far from $100 billion, maybe by  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1384120,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "2026. At some point, what happened to Nvidia \nis going to happen to Big Tech. It's going to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1390680,
    "dDurationMs": 11160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "explode. A lot more people are going to feel it.\n2023 was the moment for me where AGI went from  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1401840,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "being this theoretical, abstract thing. I see \nit, I feel it, and I see the path. I see where  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1408680,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it's going. I can see the cluster it's trained on, \nthe rough combination of algorithms, the people,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1414000,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "how it's happening. Most of the world is not there \nyet. Most of the people who feel it are right  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1418240,
    "dDurationMs": 9600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "here. A lot more of the world is going to start \nfeeling it. That's going to start being intense. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1428840,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Right now, who feels it? You can go on Twitter \nand there are these GPT wrapper companies, like,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1433160,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "\"whoa, GPT-4 is going to change our business.\"\nI'm so bearish on the wrapper companies because  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1437360,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they're betting on stagnation. They're \nbetting that you have these intermediate  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1442240,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "models and it takes so much schlep to integrate \nthem. I'm really bearish because we're just  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1446520,
    "dDurationMs": 3280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "going to sonic boom you. We're going to get the \nunhobblings. We're going to get the drop-in remote  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1449800,
    "dDurationMs": 8080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "worker. Your stuff is not going to matter.\nSo that's done. SF, this crowd, is paying  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1457880,
    "dDurationMs": 8080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "attention now. Who is going to be paying \nattention in 2026 and 2027? Presumably,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1465960,
    "dDurationMs": 3560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "these are years in which hundreds of \nbillions of capex is being spent on AI. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1470440,
    "dDurationMs": 2360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The national security state is \ngoing to start paying a lot of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1472800,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "attention. I hope we get to talk about that.\nLet’s talk about it now. What happens? What  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1479440,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is the immediate political reaction? Looking \ninternationally, I don't know if Xi Jinping  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1485680,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sees the GPT-4 news and goes, \"oh, my God, \nlook at the MMLU score on that. What are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1490280,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we doing about this, comrade?\"\nSo what happens when he sees a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1497320,
    "dDurationMs": 3200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "remote worker replacement and it has $100 \nbillion in revenue? There’s a lot of businesses  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1500520,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that have $100 billion in revenue, and people \naren't staying up all night talking about it. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1506480,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The question is, when does the CCP and when does \nthe American national security establishment  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1511880,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "realize that superintelligence is going to be \nabsolutely decisive for national power? This  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1517120,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is where the intelligence explosion stuff \ncomes in, which we should talk about later. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1521720,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You have AGI. You have this drop-in \nremote worker that can replace you or me,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1525920,
    "dDurationMs": 11200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at least for remote jobs. Fairly quickly, you \nturn the crank one or two more times and you  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1537120,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "get a thing that's smarter than humans.\nEven more than just turning the crank a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1541080,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "few more times, one of the first jobs to \nbe automated is going to be that of an  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1546800,
    "dDurationMs": 7160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "AI researcher or engineer. If you can automate \nAI research, things can start going very fast. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1554680,
    "dDurationMs": 3560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Right now, there's already at this trend \nof 0.5 OOMs a year of algorithmic progress.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1559400,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At some point, you're going to have GPU fleets \nin the tens of millions for inference or more.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1564760,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You’re going to be able to run 100 million human \nequivalents of these automated AI researchers. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1570080,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you can do that, you can maybe do a decade's \nworth of ML research progress in a year. You  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1575400,
    "dDurationMs": 8480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "get some sort of 10x speed up. You can make \nthe jump to AI that is vastly smarter than  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1583880,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "humans within a year, a couple of years.\nThat broadens from there. You have this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1589720,
    "dDurationMs": 8360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "initial acceleration of AI research. You apply \nR\u0026D to a bunch of other fields of technology. At  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1598080,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this point, you have a billion super intelligent \nresearchers, engineers, technicians, everything.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1602560,
    "dDurationMs": 3800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They’re superbly competent at all things.\nThey're going to figure out robotics. We  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1606360,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "talked about that being a software problem. Well, \nyou have a billion super smart — smarter than the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1611560,
    "dDurationMs": 3040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "smartest human researchers — AI researchers \nin your cluster. At some point during the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1614600,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "intelligence explosion, they're going to be able \nto figure out robotics. Again, that’ll expand. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1621840,
    "dDurationMs": 10120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you play this picture forward, it is fairly \nunlike any other technology. A couple years of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1631960,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lead could be utterly decisive in say, military \ncompetition. If you look at the first Gulf War,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1639080,
    "dDurationMs": 7240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Western coalition forces had a 100:1 kill ratio. \nThey had better sensors on their tanks. They had  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1646320,
    "dDurationMs": 7400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "better precision missiles, GPS, and stealth. \nThey had maybe 20-30 years of technological  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1653720,
    "dDurationMs": 8120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lead. They just completely crushed them.\nSuperintelligence applied to broad fields  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1661840,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of R\u0026D — and the industrial explosion that comes \nfrom it, robots making a lot of material — could  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1667280,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "compress a century’s worth of technological \nprogress into less than a decade. That means  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1672840,
    "dDurationMs": 10720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that a couple years could mean a Gulf War 1-style \nadvantage in military affairs. That’s including a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1683560,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "decisive advantage that even preempts nukes.\nHow do you find nuclear stealth submarines?  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1688640,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Right now, you have sensors and software to \ndetect where they are. You can do that. You  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1693280,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can find them. You have millions or billions \nof mosquito-sized drones, and they take out the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1698760,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "nuclear submarines. They take out the mobile \nlaunchers. They take out the other nukes. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1704040,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s potentially enormously destabilizing \nand enormously important for national power.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1709160,
    "dDurationMs": 9600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At some point people are going to realize \nthat. Not yet, but they will. When they do,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1718760,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it won’t just be the AI researchers in charge.\nThe CCP is going to have an all-out effort to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1725880,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "infiltrate American AI labs. It’ll involve \nbillions of dollars, thousands of people,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1729560,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the full force of the Ministry of State \nSecurity. The CCP is going to try to outbuild us. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1741200,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They added as much power in the last decade as an \nentire US electric grid. So the 100 GW cluster,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1745760,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at least the 100 GW part of it, is going to be \na lot easier for them to get. By this point,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1751040,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it's going to be an extremely \nintense international competition. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1758840,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One thing I'm uncertain about in this picture \nis if it’s like what you say, where it's more  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1763600,
    "dDurationMs": 8200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of an explosion. You’ve developed an AGI. You \nmake it into an AI researcher. For a while,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1772680,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you're only using this ability to make hundreds \nof millions of other AI researchers. The thing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1779400,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that comes out of this really frenetic process \nis a superintelligence. Then that goes out in the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1785200,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "world and is developing robotics and helping \nyou take over other countries and whatever. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1792560,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It's a little bit more gradual. It's an \nexplosion that starts narrowly. It can do  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1796000,
    "dDurationMs": 3400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cognitive jobs. The highest ROI use for \ncognitive jobs is to make the AI better  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1799400,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and solve robotics. As you solve robotics, now \nyou can do R\u0026D in biology and other technology. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1806920,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Initially, you start with the factory workers. \nThey're wearing the glasses and AirPods, and the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1811160,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "AI is instructing them because you can make any \nworker into a skilled technician. Then you have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1815000,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the robots come in. So this process expands.\nMeta's Ray-Bans are a complement to Llama. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1821960,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "With the fabs in the US, their constraint is \nskilled workers. Even if you don't have robots,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1827160,
    "dDurationMs": 2840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you have the cognitive superintelligence \nand can kind of make them all into skilled  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1830000,
    "dDurationMs": 2880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "workers immediately. That's a very \nbrief period. Robots will come soon. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1834240,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Suppose this is actually how the tech \nprogresses in the United States, maybe  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1838760,
    "dDurationMs": 4040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because these companies are already generating \nhundreds of billions of dollars of AI revenue "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1842800,
    "dDurationMs": 3880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At this point, companies are borrowing hundreds \nof billions or more in the corporate debt markets. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1847320,
    "dDurationMs": 4040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Why is a CCP bureaucrat, some 60-year-old \nguy, looking at this and going,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1851360,
    "dDurationMs": 1520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "\"oh, Copilot has gotten better now\" and now—\nThis is much more than Copilot has gotten  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1852880,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "better now.\nIt’d require  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1861920,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "shifting the production of an entire country, \ndislocating energy that is otherwise being used  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1868120,
    "dDurationMs": 9680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for consumer goods or something, and feeding all \nthat into the data centers. Part of this whole  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1877800,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "story is that you realize superintelligence is \ncoming soon. You realize it and maybe I realize  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1883320,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it. I'm not sure how much I realize it.\nWill the national security apparatus in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1887560,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the United States and the CCP realize it?\nThis is a really key question. We have a few  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1895080,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more years of mid-game. We have a few more \n2023s. That just starts updating more and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1899680,
    "dDurationMs": 10760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more people. The trend lines will become clear.\nYou will see some amount of the COVID dynamic.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1910440,
    "dDurationMs": 8360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "COVID in February of 2020 honestly feels a lot \nlike today. It feels like this utterly crazy thing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1918800,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is coming. You see the exponential and yet most \nof the world just doesn't realize it. The mayor of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1925640,
    "dDurationMs": 10800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "New York is like, \"go out to the shows,\" and \"this \nis just Asian racism.\" At some point, people saw  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1936440,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it and then crazy, radical reactions came.\nBy the way, what were you doing during  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1943480,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "COVID? Was it your freshman or sophomore year?\nJunior. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1949600,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Still, you were like a 17-year-old junior or \nsomething right? Did you short the market or  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1956880,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "something? Did you sell at the right time?\nYeah. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1962240,
    "dDurationMs": 8720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So there will be a March 2020 moment.\nYou can make the analogy you make in the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1970960,
    "dDurationMs": 7320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "series that this will cause a reaction like, “we \nhave to do the Manhattan Project again for America  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1978280,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "here.” I wonder what the politics of this will \nbe like. The difference here is that it’s not  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1983960,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just like, “we need the bomb to beat the Nazis.”\nWe'll be building this thing that makes all our  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1989760,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "energy prices go up a bunch and it's automating a \nlot of our jobs. The climate change stuff people  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1994800,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are going to be like, \"oh, my God, it's making \nclimate change worse and it's helping Big Tech.\" "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1999280,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Politically, this doesn't seem like a dynamic \nwhere the national security apparatus or the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2003200,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "president is like, \"we have to step on \nthe gas here and make sure America wins.\" "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2010200,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Again, a lot of this really depends on how \nmuch people are feeling it and how much people  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2013960,
    "dDurationMs": 14720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are seeing it. Our generation is so used to \npeace, American hegemony and nothing matters.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2031280,
    "dDurationMs": 3320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The historical norm is very much one of extremely \nintense and extraordinary things happening in the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2034600,
    "dDurationMs": 14840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "world with intense international competition.\nThere's a 20-year very unique period. In World  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2049440,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "War II, something like 50% of GDP went \nto war production. The US borrowed over  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2054320,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "60% of GDP. With Germany and Japan I think \nit was over 100%. In World War I, the UK,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2061920,
    "dDurationMs": 11440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "France, and Germany all borrowed over 100% of GDP.\nMuch more was on the line. People talk about  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2073360,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "World War I being so destructive with 20 \nmillion Soviet soldiers dying and 20% of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2078080,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Poland. That happened all the time. During \nthe Seven Years' War something like 20-30%  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2083600,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of Prussia died. In the Thirty Years' War, \nup to 50% of a large swath of Germany died. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2098680,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Will people see that the stakes here are \nreally high and that history is actually  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2104360,
    "dDurationMs": 7080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "back? The American national security state \nthinks very seriously about stuff like this.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2111440,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They think very seriously about competition with \nChina. China very much thinks of itself on this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2115280,
    "dDurationMs": 3240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "historical mission of the rejuvenation of the \nChinese nation. They think a lot about national  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2118520,
    "dDurationMs": 7400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "power. They think a lot about the world order.\nThere's a real question on timing. Do they  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2125920,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "start taking this seriously when the intelligence \nexplosion is already happening quite late. Do they  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2130280,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "start taking this seriously two years earlier? \nThat matters a lot for how things play out. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2134120,
    "dDurationMs": 8480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At some point they will and they will realize \nthat this will be utterly decisive for not  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2142600,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just some proxy war but for major questions. \nCan liberal democracy continue to thrive? Can  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2148120,
    "dDurationMs": 8120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the CCP continue existing? That will activate \nforces that we haven't seen in a long time. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2156240,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The great power conflict definitely seems \ncompelling. All kinds of different things  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2162160,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "seem much more likely when you think \nfrom a historical perspective. You  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2165600,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "zoom out beyond the liberal democracy that \nwe’ve had the pleasure to live in America  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2170040,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for say the last 80 years. That includes \nthings like dictatorships, war, famine, etc. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2176920,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I was reading The Gulag Archipelago and one of the \nchapters begins with Solzhenitsyn saying how if  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2181520,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you had told a Russian citizen under the tsars \nthat because of all these new technologies — we  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2186640,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "wouldn’t see some Great Russian revival with \nRussia becoming a great power and the citizens  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2191720,
    "dDurationMs": 7560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "made wealthy — you would see tens of millions of \nSoviet citizens tortured by millions of beasts in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2199280,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the worst possible ways. If you’d told them that \nthat would be the result of the 20th century,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2204080,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they wouldn’t have believed you. \nThey’d have called you a slanderer. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2210160,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The possibilities for dictatorship with \nsuperintelligence are even crazier as well.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2215080,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Imagine you have a perfectly loyal military \nand security force. No more rebellions. No  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2220160,
    "dDurationMs": 7720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more popular uprisings. You have perfect lie \ndetection. You have surveillance of everybody.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2227880,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You can perfectly figure out who's the dissenter \nand weed them out. No Gorbachev who had some  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2232720,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "doubts about the system would have ever risen to \npower. No military coup would have ever happened. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2238840,
    "dDurationMs": 9200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There's a real way in which part of why things \nhave worked out is that ideas can evolve. There's  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2248840,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "some sense in which time heals a lot of wounds and \nsolves a lot of debates. Throughout time, a lot of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2254760,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "people had really strong convictions, but a lot \nof those have been overturned over time because  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2258200,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there's been continued pluralism and evolution.\nImagine applying a CCP-like approach to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2264200,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "truth where truth is what the party says. When \nyou supercharge that with superintelligence, that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2268800,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "could just be locked in and enshrined for a long \ntime. The possibilities are pretty terrifying. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2276440,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To your point about history and living in America \nfor the past eight years, this is one of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2283080,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "things I took away from growing up in Germany. A \nlot of this stuff feels more visceral. My mother  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2288040,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "grew up in the former East, my father in the \nformer West. They met shortly after the Wall fell.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2292360,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The end of the Cold War was this extremely pivotal \nmoment for me because it's the reason I exist. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2296760,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I grew up in Berlin with the former Wall. \nMy great-grandmother, who is still alive,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2304520,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is very important in my life. She was born in 1934 \nand grew up during the Nazi era. In World War II,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2312120,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "she saw the firebombing of Dresden from \nthis country cottage where they were as  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2317200,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "kids. Then she spent most of her life in \nthe East German communist dictatorship. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2322960,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "She'd tell me about how Soviet tanks came \nwhen there was the popular uprising in 1954.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2328760,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Her husband was telling her to get home \nreally quickly and get off the streets.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2332960,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "She had a son who tried to ride a motorcycle \nacross the Iron Curtain and then was put in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2338840,
    "dDurationMs": 7640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a Stasi prison for a while. Finally, when \nshe's almost 60, it was the first time she  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2346480,
    "dDurationMs": 10600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lived in a free country, and a wealthy country.\nWhen I was a kid, the thing she always really  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2357080,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "didn't want me to do was get involved in \npolitics. Joining a political party had  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2362480,
    "dDurationMs": 8160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "very bad connotations for her. She raised \nme when I was young. So it doesn't feel  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2370640,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that long ago. It feels very close.\nThere’s one thing I wonder about when  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2374240,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we're talking today about the CCP. The people \nin China who will be doing their version of this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2380760,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "project will be AI researchers who are somewhat \nWesternized. They’ll either have gotten educated  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2388200,
    "dDurationMs": 9240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the West or have colleagues in the West.\nAre they going to sign up for the CCP  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2397440,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "project that's going to hand over control to Xi \nJinping? What's your sense of that? Fundamentally,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2403640,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they're just people, right? Can't you convince \nthem about the dangers of superintelligence? "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2407160,
    "dDurationMs": 6680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Will they be in charge though? In some \nsense, this is also the case in the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2413840,
    "dDurationMs": 3880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "US. This is like the rapidly depreciating \ninfluence of the lab employees. Right now,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2417720,
    "dDurationMs": 6920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the AI lab employees have so much power. You \nsaw this November event. It’s so much power. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2425560,
    "dDurationMs": 3240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Both are going to get automated and they're \ngoing to lose all their power. It'll just be  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2429400,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a few people in charge with their armies of \nautomated AIs. It’s also the politicians and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2435520,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the generals and the national security \nstate. There are some of these classic  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2440000,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "scenes from the Oppenheimer movie. The \nscientists built it and then the bomb was  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2444480,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "shipped away and it was out of their hands.\nIt's good for lab employees to be aware of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2450480,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this. You have a lot of power now, but \nmaybe not for that long. Use it wisely.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2458560,
    "dDurationMs": 3400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I do think they would benefit from some \nmore organs of representative democracy. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2461960,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What do you mean by that?\nIn the OpenAI board events,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2465560,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "employee power is exercised in a very direct \ndemocracy way. How some of that went about  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2471160,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "really highlighted the benefits of representative \ndemocracy and having some deliberative organs. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2474880,
    "dDurationMs": 9200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Interesting. Let's go back to the $100 billion \nrevenue question. The companies are trying to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2484080,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "build clusters that are this big. Where \nare they building it? Say it's the amount  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2488440,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of energy that would be required for a small or \nmedium-sized US state. Does Colorado then get no  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2493080,
    "dDurationMs": 2720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "power because it's happening in the United \nStates? Is it happening somewhere else? "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2497640,
    "dDurationMs": 2960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is the thing that I always find funny, \nwhen you talk about Colorado getting no power.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2500600,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The easy way to get the power would be to \ndisplace less economically useful stuff.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2505280,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Buy up the aluminum smelting plant that has a \ngigawatt. We're going to replace it with the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2509520,
    "dDurationMs": 3800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "data center because that's important. That's not \nactually happening because a lot of these power  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2513320,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "contracts are really locked in long-term. \nAlso, people don't like things like this. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2519080,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In practice what it requires, at least \nright now, is building new power. That  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2523280,
    "dDurationMs": 3040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "might change. That's when things get really \ninteresting, when it's like, “no, we're just  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2526320,
    "dDurationMs": 3800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dedicating all of the power to the AGI.”\nSo right now it's building new power. 10  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2530120,
    "dDurationMs": 8440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "GW is quite doable. It's like a few percent of US \nnatural gas production. When you have the 10 GW  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2538560,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "training cluster, you have a lot more inference. \n100 gigawatts is where it starts getting pretty  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2543200,
    "dDurationMs": 7080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "wild. That's over 20% of US electricity \nproduction. It's pretty doable, especially  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2550280,
    "dDurationMs": 6760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if you're willing to go for natural gas.\nIt is incredibly important that these  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2557040,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "clusters are in the United States.\nWhy does it matter that it's in the US? "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2563600,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are some people who are trying to \nbuild clusters elsewhere. There's a lot of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2568680,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "free-flowing Middle Eastern money that's trying \nto build clusters elsewhere. This comes back to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2574840,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the national security question we talked about. \nWould you do the Manhattan Project in the UAE? "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2579560,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You can put the clusters in the US and you can \nput them in allied democracies. Once you put them  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2587320,
    "dDurationMs": 6680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in authoritarian dictatorships, you create this \nirreversible security risk. Once the cluster is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2594000,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there, it's much easier for them to exfiltrate \nthe weights. They can literally steal the AGI,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2598240,
    "dDurationMs": 6920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the superintelligence. It’s like they got a direct \ncopy of the atomic bomb. It makes it much easier  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2605160,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for them. They have weird ties to China. They \ncan ship that to China. That's a huge risk. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2609360,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Another thing is they can just seize the compute. \nThe issue here is people right now are thinking  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2615320,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of this as ChatGPT, Big Tech product \nclusters. The clusters being planned now,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2619640,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "three to five years out, may well be the AGI, \nsuperintelligence clusters. When things get hot,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2625680,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they might just seize the compute.\nSuppose we put 25% of the compute  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2630120,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "capacity in these Middle Eastern dictatorships. \nSay they seize that. Now it's a ratio of compute  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2634680,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of 3:1. We still have more, but even with \nonly 25% of compute there it starts getting  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2641000,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pretty hairy. 3:1 is not that great of a ratio. \nYou can do a lot with that amount of compute. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2646560,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Say they don't actually do this. Even if \nthey don't actually seize the compute,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2649680,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even if they actually don't steal the weights, \nthere's just a lot of implicit leverage you  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2653520,
    "dDurationMs": 8040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "get. They get seats at the AGI table. I \ndon't know why we're giving authoritarian  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2661560,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dictatorships the seat at the AGI table.\nThere's going to be a lot of compute  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2666840,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the Middle East if these deals go through.\nFirst of all, who is it? Is it just every  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2671160,
    "dDurationMs": 4507,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "single Big Tech company trying \nto figure it out over there? "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2675667,
    "dDurationMs": 733,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s not everybody, some.\nThere are reports, I think  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2676400,
    "dDurationMs": 6760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Microsoft. We'll get into it.\nSo say the UAE gets a bunch of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2683160,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "compute because we're building the clusters there. \nLet's say they have 25% of the compute. Why does a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2689800,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "compute ratio matter? If it's about them being \nable to kick off the intelligence explosion,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2696640,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "isn't it just some threshold where you have \n100 million AI researchers or you don't? "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2701120,
    "dDurationMs": 8840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You can do a lot with 33 million extremely \nsmart scientists. That might be enough  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2709960,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to build the crazy bio weapons. Then \nyou're in a situation where they stole  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2713720,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the weights and they seized the compute.\nNow they can make these crazy new WMDs that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2719320,
    "dDurationMs": 3280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "will be possible with superintelligence. \nNow you've just proliferated the stuff  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2722600,
    "dDurationMs": 7240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that’ll be really powerful. Also, 3x \non compute isn't actually that much. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2729840,
    "dDurationMs": 15360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The riskiest situation is if we're in some sort \nof really neck and neck, feverish international  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2745200,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "struggle. Say we're really close with the CCP \nand we're months apart. The situation we want  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2751600,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to be in — and could be in if we play our cards \nright — is a little bit more like the US building  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2755840,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the atomic bomb versus the German project years \nbehind. If we have that, we just have so much  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2762240,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more wiggle room to get safety right.\nWe're going to be building these crazy  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2765600,
    "dDurationMs": 8120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "new WMDs that completely undermine nuclear \ndeterrence. That's so much easier to deal  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2773720,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with if you don't have somebody right on your \ntails and you have to go at maximum speed.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2778840,
    "dDurationMs": 4040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You have no wiggle room. You're worried \nthat at any time they can overtake you. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2782880,
    "dDurationMs": 4040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They can also just try to outbuild you. They \nmight literally win. China might literally win  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2787840,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if they can steal the weights, because they \ncan outbuild you. They may have less caution,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2792600,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "both good and bad caution in terms of \nwhatever unreasonable regulations we have. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2800680,
    "dDurationMs": 3240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you're in this really tight race, this \nsort of feverish struggle, that's when  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2803920,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there's the greatest peril of self-destruction.\nPresumably the companies that are trying to build  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2809720,
    "dDurationMs": 3640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "clusters in the Middle East realize this. \nIs it just that it’s impossible to do this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2813360,
    "dDurationMs": 2649,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in America? If you want American companies \nto do this at all, do you have to do it in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2816009,
    "dDurationMs": 4191,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Middle East or not at all? Then you just \nhave China build a Three Gorges Dam cluster. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2820200,
    "dDurationMs": 2600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There’s a few reasons. People \naren’t thinking about this as the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2822800,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "AGI superintelligence cluster. They’re just \nlike, “ah, cool clusters for my ChatGPT.” "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2832920,
    "dDurationMs": 2600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you’re doing ones for inference, presumably \nyou could spread them out across the country or  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2835520,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "something. The ones they’re building, \nthey’re going to do one training run  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2839880,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in a single thing they’re building.\nIt’s just hard to distinguish between  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2843640,
    "dDurationMs": 2480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "inference and training compute. People \ncan claim it’s inference compute,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2846120,
    "dDurationMs": 7080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but they might realize that actually this is \ngoing to be useful for training compute too. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2853200,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Because of synthetic data and things like that?\nRL looks a lot like inference, for example. Or  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2858520,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you just end up connecting them in time. It's a \nlot like raw materials. It's like placing your  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2864520,
    "dDurationMs": 3400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "uranium refinement facilities there.\nSo there are a few reasons. One,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2867920,
    "dDurationMs": 2360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they don't think about this as the AGI \ncluster. Another is just that there’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2870280,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "easy money coming from the Middle East.\nAnother one is that some people think  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2877000,
    "dDurationMs": 7080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that you can't do it in the US. We actually \nface a real system competition here. Some  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2884080,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "people think that only autocracies that can do \nthis with top-down mobilization of industrial  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2888400,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "capacity and the power to get stuff done fast.\nAgain, this is the sort of thing we haven't faced  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2893880,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in a while. But during the Cold War, there was \nthis intense system competition. East vs. West  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2899800,
    "dDurationMs": 7880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Germany was this. It was West Germany as liberal \ndemocratic capitalism vs. state-planned communism. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2907680,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now it's obvious that the free world \nwould win. But even as late as 1961,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2913160,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Paul Samuelson was predicting that the Soviet \nUnion would outgrow the United States because  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2916920,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they were able to mobilize industry better.\nSo there are some people who shitpost about  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2923240,
    "dDurationMs": 3640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "loving America, but then in private they're \nbetting against America. They're betting against  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2926880,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the liberal order. Basically, it's just a bad \nbet. This stuff is really possible in the US. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2933760,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To make it possible in the US, to some degree we \nhave to get our act together. There are basically  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2938360,
    "dDurationMs": 4040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "two paths to doing it in the US. One is you just \nhave to be willing to do natural gas. There's  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2942400,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ample natural gas. You put your cluster in West \nTexas. You put it in southwest Pennsylvania by  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2946800,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Marcellus Shale. The 10 GW cluster is super \neasy. The 100 GW cluster is also pretty doable.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2953680,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I think natural gas production in the United \nStates has almost doubled in a decade. You do  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2957920,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that one more time over the next seven years, you \ncould power multiple trillion-dollar data centers. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2965320,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The issue there is that a lot of people made these \nclimate commitments, not just the government. It's  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2969560,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "actually the private companies themselves, \nMicrosoft, Amazon, etc., that have made  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2973280,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "these climate commitments. So they won't do \nnatural gas. I admire the climate commitments,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2977960,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but at some point the national interest \nand national security is more important. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2983920,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The other path is doing green energy \nmegaprojects. You do solar and batteries  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2988480,
    "dDurationMs": 8040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and SMRs and geothermal. If we want to do that, \nthere needs to be a broad deregulatory push.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2997560,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You can't have permitting take a decade. You \nhave to reform FERC. You have to have blanket  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3002040,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "NEPA exemptions for this stuff.\nThere are inane state-level regulations. You can  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3007720,
    "dDurationMs": 3320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "build the solar panels and batteries next to your \ndata center, but it'll still take years because  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3011040,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you actually have to hook it up to the state \nelectrical grid. You have to use governmental  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3018080,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "powers to create rights of way to have multiple \nclusters and connect them and have the cables. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3025280,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Ideally we do both. Ideally we do natural gas \nand the broader deregulatory green agenda.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3028720,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We have to do at least one. Then this \nstuff is possible in the United States. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3036480,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Before the conversation I was reading a good book \nabout World War II industrial mobilization in the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3041600,
    "dDurationMs": 7672,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "United States called Freedom's Forge. I’m thinking \nback on that period, especially in the context of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3049272,
    "dDurationMs": 3565,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "reading Patrick Collison’s Fast and the progress \nstudy stuff. There’s this narrative out there that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3052837,
    "dDurationMs": 7123,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we had state capacity back then and people just \ngot shit done but that now it's a clusterfuck. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3059960,
    "dDurationMs": 1680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It wasn’t at all the case!\nIt was really interesting. You had  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3061640,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "people from the Detroit auto industry side, like \nWilliam Knudsen, who were running mobilization for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3068240,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the United States. They were extremely competent. \nAt the same time you had labor organization and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3074160,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "agitation, which is very analogous to the climate \nchange pledges and concerns we have today. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3081160,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They would literally have these strikes, \ninto 1941, costing millions of man-hours  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3088680,
    "dDurationMs": 8000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "worth of time when we're trying to make tens \nof thousands of planes a month. They would just  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3096680,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "debilitate factories for trivial concessions \nfrom capital that were pennies on the dollar. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3103000,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There were concerns that the auto companies \nwere trying to use the pretext of a potential  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3110600,
    "dDurationMs": 7560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "war to prevent paying labor the money it \ndeserves. So with what climate change is today,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3118160,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you might think, \"ah, America's fucked. We're \nnot going to be able to build this shit if you  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3122160,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "look at NEPA or something,” I didn't realize \nhow debilitating labor was in World War II. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3126760,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It wasn’ just that. Before 1939, the American \nmilitary was in total shambles. You read about  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3132400,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it and it reads a little bit like the German \nmilitary today. Military expenditures were I think  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3137240,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "less than 2% of GDP. All the European countries \nhad gone, even in peacetime, above 10% of GDP. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3142640,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It was rapid mobilization starting \nfrom nothing. We were making no planes.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3146640,
    "dDurationMs": 3480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There were no military contracts. Everything \nhad been starved during the Great Depression.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3150120,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But there was this latent capacity. At some \npoint the United States got its act together. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3155760,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This applies the other way around too with China. \nSometimes people count them out a little bit with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3163280,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the export controls and so on. They're able to \nmake 7-nanometer chips now. There's a question  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3168120,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of how many they could make. There's at least \na possibility that they're going to mature that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3172600,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ability and make a lot of 7-nanometer chips.\nThere's a lot of latent industrial capacity  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3177200,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in China. They are able to build a lot of power \nfast. Maybe that isn't activated for AI yet. At  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3182680,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "some point, the same way the United States and \na lot of people in the US government are going  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3188280,
    "dDurationMs": 15240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to wake up, the CCP is going to wake up.\nCompanies realize that scaling is a thing.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3203520,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Obviously their whole plans are contingent on \nscaling. So they understand that in 2028 we're  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3207720,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "going to be building 10 GW data centers.\nAt that point, the people who can keep up  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3213440,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are Big Tech, potentially at the edge of their \ncapabilities, sovereign wealth fund-funded things,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3220640,
    "dDurationMs": 9920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and also major countries like America and China. \nWhat's their plan? With the AI labs, what's their  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3230560,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "plan given this landscape? Do they not want \nthe leverage of being in the United States? "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3238240,
    "dDurationMs": 7000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Middle East does offer capital, but America \nhas plenty of capital. We have trillion-dollar  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3245240,
    "dDurationMs": 2320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "companies. What are these Middle Eastern \nstates? They're kind of like trillion-dollar  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3247560,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "oil companies. We have trillion-dollar companies \nand very deep financial markets. Microsoft could  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3252440,
    "dDurationMs": 3280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "issue hundreds of billions of dollars of \nbonds and they can pay for these clusters. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3256720,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Another argument being made, which is worth \ntaking seriously, is that if we don't work  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3261720,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with the UAE or with these Middle Eastern \ncountries, they're just going to go to China.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3268080,
    "dDurationMs": 3200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They're going to build data centers and \npour money into AI regardless. If we don't  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3271280,
    "dDurationMs": 9240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "work with them, they'll just support China.\nThere's some merit to the argument in the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3280520,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sense that we should be doing benefit-sharing \nwith them. On the road to AGI, there should be  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3286720,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "two tiers of coalitions. There should be a narrow \ncoalition of democracies that's developing AGI.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3292480,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Then there should be a broader coalition of other \ncountries, including dictatorships, and we should  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3300440,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "offer them some of the benefits of AI.\nIf the UAE wants to use AI products,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3305680,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "run Meta recommendation engines, \nor run the last-generation models,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3310280,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that's fine. By default, they just wouldn't \nhave had this seat at the AGI table. So they  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3315880,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have some money, but a lot of people have money.\nThe only reason they're getting this seat at the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3322360,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "AGI table and giving these dictators this leverage \nover this extremely important national security  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3329400,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "technology, is because we're getting \nthem excited and offering it to them. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3339080,
    "dDurationMs": 2920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Who specifically is doing this? Who are the \ncompanies who are going there to fundraise? "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3344760,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s been reported that Sam Altman is trying \nto raise $7 trillion or whatever for a chip  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3349840,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "project. It's unclear how many of the clusters \nwill be there, but definitely stuff is happening. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3355640,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There’s another reason I'm a little suspicious \nof this argument that if the US doesn't work  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3360720,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with them, they'll go to China. I've heard from \nmultiple people — not from my time at OpenAI,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3367680,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and I haven't seen the memo — that at some \npoint several years ago, OpenAI leadership  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3374000,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "had laid out a plan to fund and sell AGI by \nstarting a bidding war between the governments  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3378560,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the United States, China, and Russia.\nIt's surprising to me that they're willing to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3384080,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sell AGI to the Chinese and Russian governments. \nThere's also something that feels eerily familiar  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3389080,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "about starting this bidding war and then \nplaying them off each other, saying, \"well,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3393000,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if you don't do this, China will do it.\"\nInteresting. That's pretty fucked up. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3401800,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Suppose you're right. We ended up in this place \nbecause, as one of our friends put it, the Middle  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3408200,
    "dDurationMs": 3880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "East has billions or trillions of dollars up \nfor persuasion like no other place in the world. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3413200,
    "dDurationMs": 9880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "With little accountability. There’s no \nMicrosoft board. It's only the dictator. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3424200,
    "dDurationMs": 2640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let's say you're right, that you shouldn't \nhave gotten them excited about AGI in the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3426840,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "first place. Now we're in a place where they \nare excited about AGI and they're like, \"fuck,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3432600,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we want to have GPT-5 while you're going to be off \nbuilding superintelligence. This Atoms for Peace  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3436320,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "thing doesn't work for us.\" If you're in this \nplace, don't they already have the leverage? "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3445360,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The UAE on its own is not competitive. They're \nalready export-controlled. You're not supposed  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3451640,
    "dDurationMs": 3240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to ship Nvidia chips over there. It's \nnot like they have any of the leading  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3454880,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "AI labs. They have money, but it's hard \nto just translate money into progress. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3458640,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But I want to go back to other things you've \nbeen saying in laying out your vision. There's  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3462360,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this almost industrial process of putting in \nthe compute and algorithms, adding that up,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3468160,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and getting AGI on the other end. If it's \nsomething more like that, then the case for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3475040,
    "dDurationMs": 3560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "somebody being able to catch up rapidly seems \nmore compelling than if it's some bespoke... "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3478600,
    "dDurationMs": 7000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, if they can steal the algorithms and if they \ncan steal the weights, that’s really important. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3489120,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "How easy would it be for an actor to steal \nthe things that are not the trivial released  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3495440,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "things, like Scarlett Johansson's voice, but the \nRL things we're talking about, the unhobblings? "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3500480,
    "dDurationMs": 7960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s all extremely easy. They don’t make the claim \nthat it’s hard. DeepMind put out their Frontier  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3508440,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Safety Framework and they lay out security levels, \nzero to four. Four is resistant to state activity.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3514640,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They say, we're at level zero. Just recently, \nthere was an indictment of a guy who stole a bunch  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3521040,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of really important AI code and went to China \nwith it. All he had to do to steal the code was  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3526240,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "copy it, put it into Apple Notes, and export \nit as a PDF. That got past their monitoring. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3531600,
    "dDurationMs": 3480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Google has the best security of any of the AI \nlabs probably, because they have the Google  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3535080,
    "dDurationMs": 6920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "infrastructure. I would think of the security of \na startup. What does security of a startup look  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3542000,
    "dDurationMs": 7000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like? It's not that good. It's easy to steal.\nEven if that's the case, a lot of your  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3549000,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "post is making the argument for why we are going \nto get the intelligence explosion. If we have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3555040,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "somebody with the intuition of an Alec Radford to \ncome up with all these ideas, that intuition is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3559960,
    "dDurationMs": 12360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "extremely valuable and you can scale that up.\nIf it's just intuition, then that's not going  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3572320,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to be just in the code, right? Also because \nof export controls, these countries are going  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3576160,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to have slightly different hardware. You're going \nto have to make different trade-offs and probably  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3581120,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rewrite things to be compatible with that.\nIs it just a matter of getting the right pen  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3587400,
    "dDurationMs": 3080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "drive and plugging it into the gigawatt \ndata center next to the Three Gorges Dam  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3590480,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and then you're off to the races?\nThere are a few different things,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3594160,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "right? One threat model is just them stealing \nthe weights themselves. The weights one is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3598920,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particularly insane because they can just steal \nthe literal end product — just make a replica of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3603800,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the atomic bomb — and then they're ready to go. \nThat one is extremely important around the time  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3609360,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we have AGI and superintelligence because China \ncan build a big cluster by default. We'd have a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3615840,
    "dDurationMs": 3160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "big lead because we have the better scientists, \nbut if we make the superintelligence and they  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3619000,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just steal it, they're off to the races.\nWeights are a little bit less important right now  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3623400,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because who cares if they steal the GPT-4 weights. \nWe still have to get started on weight security  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3630520,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "now because if we think there’s AGI by 2027, this \nstuff is going to take a while. It's not just  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3635560,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "going to be like, \"oh, we do some access control.\" \nIf you actually want to be resistant to Chinese  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3639880,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "espionage, it needs to be much more intense.\nThe thing that people aren't paying enough  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3645600,
    "dDurationMs": 13320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "attention to is the secrets. The compute stuff is \nsexy, but people underrate the secrets. The half  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3658920,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an order of magnitude a year is just by default, \nsort of algorithmic progress. That's huge. If we  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3662360,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have a few years of lead, by default, that's a \n10-30x, 100x bigger cluster, if we protect them. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3669080,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There's this additional layer of the data wall. \nWe have to get through the data wall. That means  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3672680,
    "dDurationMs": 3000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we actually have to figure out some sort of \nbasic new paradigm. So it’s the “AlphaGo step  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3675680,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "two.” “AlphaGo step one” learns from human \nimitation. “AlphaGo step two” is the kind  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3680080,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of self-play RL thing that everyone's working \non right now. Maybe we're going to crack it.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3687040,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If China can't steal that, then they're stuck. \nIf they can steal it, they're off to the races. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3693360,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Whatever that thing is, can I literally write it \ndown on the back of a napkin? If it's that easy,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3699320,
    "dDurationMs": 3160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then why is it so hard for them to figure \nit out? If it's more about the intuitions,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3702480,
    "dDurationMs": 3320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then don't you just have to hire Alec \nRadford? What are you copying down? "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3705800,
    "dDurationMs": 9520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are a few layers to this. At the top is the \nfundamental approach. On pre-training it might be  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3716640,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "unsupervised learning, next token prediction, \ntraining on the entire Internet. You actually  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3720240,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "get a lot of juice out of that already. \nThat one's very quick to communicate. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3724080,
    "dDurationMs": 3280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Then there's a lot of details that matter, \nand you were talking about this earlier.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3727360,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It's probably going to be somewhat obvious in \nretrospect, or there's going to be some not too  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3734240,
    "dDurationMs": 3320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "complicated thing that'll work, but there's \ngoing to be a lot of details to get that. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3737560,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If that's true, then again, why do we \nthink that getting state-level security  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3743000,
    "dDurationMs": 2920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in these startups will prevent China \nfrom catching up? It’s just like, \"oh,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3745920,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we know some sort of self-play RL will \nbe required to get past the data wall.\" "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3753040,
    "dDurationMs": 3320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It's going to be solved by \n2027, right? It's not that hard. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3756920,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The US, and the leading labs in the United States, \nhave this huge lead. By default, China actually  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3762400,
    "dDurationMs": 7920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "has some good LLMs because they're just using open \nsource code, like Llama. People really underrate  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3770840,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "both the divergence on algorithmic progress and \nthe lead the US would have by default because  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3775520,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all this stuff was published until recently.\nLook at Chinchilla Scaling laws, MoE papers,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3780760,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "transformers. All that stuff was published. \nThat's why open source is good and why China  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3785000,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can make some good models. Now, they're \nnot publishing it anymore. If we actually  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3790520,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "kept it secret, it would be a huge edge.\nTo your point about tacit knowledge and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3795600,
    "dDurationMs": 3880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Alec Radford, there's another layer at the \nbottom that is something about large-scale  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3799480,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "engineering work to make these big training \nruns work. That is a little bit more like  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3804320,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tacit knowledge, but China will be able to figure \nthat out. It's engineering schlep, and they're  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3807920,
    "dDurationMs": 1720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "going to figure out how to do it.\nWhy can't they figure that out,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3809640,
    "dDurationMs": 7160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but not how to get the RL thing working?\nI don't know. Germany during World War II  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3816800,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "went down the wrong path with heavy \nwater. There's an amazing anecdote  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3821920,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in The Making of the Atomic Bomb about this.\nSecrecy was one of the most contentious issues  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3826360,
    "dDurationMs": 10120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "early on. Leo Szilard really thought a nuclear \nchain reaction and an atomic bomb were possible.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3836480,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He went around saying, \"this is going to be of \nenormous strategic and military importance.\"  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3841640,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A lot of people didn't believe it or thought, \n\"maybe this is possible, but I'm going to act  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3845080,
    "dDurationMs": 8560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as though it's not, and science should be open.\"\nIn the early days, there had been some incorrect  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3853640,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "measurements made on graphite as a moderator. \nGermany thought graphite wasn't going to work,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3860000,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so they had to do heavy water. But then Enrico \nFermi made new measurements indicating that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3867200,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "graphite would work. This was really important.\nSzilard assaulted Fermi with another secrecy  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3873760,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "appeal and Fermi was pissed off, throwing a \ntemper tantrum. He thought it was absurd, saying,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3879840,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "\"come on, this is crazy.\" But Szilard persisted, \nand they roped in another guy, George Pegram.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3885920,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the end, Fermi didn't publish it.\nThat was just in time. Fermi not publishing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3891600,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "meant that the Nazis didn't figure out graphite \nwould work. They went down the path of heavy  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3895840,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "water, which was the wrong path. \nThis is a key reason why the German  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3899920,
    "dDurationMs": 8040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "project didn't work out. They were way behind.\nWe face a similar situation now. Are we just  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3907960,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "going to instantly leak how to get past the data \nwall and what the next paradigm is? Or are we not? "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3913360,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The reason this would matter is if being \none year ahead would be a huge advantage.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3918400,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the world where you deploy AI over time \nthey're just going to catch up anyway. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3923440,
    "dDurationMs": 2200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I interviewed Richard \nRhodes, the guy who wrote The  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3929040,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Making of the Atomic Bomb. One of the anecdotes \nhe had was when the Soviets realized America had  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3933880,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the bomb. Obviously, we dropped it in Japan.\nLavrentiy Beria — the guy who ran the NKVD,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3940600,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a famously ruthless and evil guy — goes to the \nSoviet scientist who was running their version  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3946840,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the Manhattan Project. He says, \"comrade, you \nwill get us the American bomb.\" The guy says,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3953400,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "\"well, listen, their implosion device actually is \nnot optimal. We should make it a different way.\"  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3957240,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Beria says, \"no, you will get us the American \nbomb, or your family will be camp dust.\" "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3963760,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The thing that's relevant about that anecdote is \nthat the Soviets would have had a better bomb if  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3969440,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they hadn't copied the American design, at least \ninitially. That suggests something about history,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3975240,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not just for the Manhattan Project. There's \noften this pattern of parallel invention  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3980840,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because the tech tree implies that a certain \nthing is next — in this case, a self-play  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3986960,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "RL — and people work on that and are going to \nfigure it out around the same time. There's not  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3992720,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "going to be that much gap in who gets it first.\nFamously, a bunch of people invented the light  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3997880,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bulb around the same time. Is it the case \nthat it might be true but the one year  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4003560,
    "dDurationMs": 2280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or six months makes the difference?\nTwo years makes all the difference. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4005840,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I don't know if it'll be two years though.\nIf we lock down the labs, we have much better  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4010680,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "scientists. We're way ahead. It would \nbe two years. Even six months, a year,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4016160,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would make a huge difference. This gets back \nto the intelligence explosion dynamics. A year  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4019520,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "might be the difference between a system that's \nsort of human-level and a system that is vastly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4025200,
    "dDurationMs": 8560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "superhuman. It might be like five OOMs.\nLook at the current pace. Three years ago,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4033760,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on the math benchmark — these are really \ndifficult high school competition math  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4041200,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "problems — we were at a few percent, we couldn't \nsolve anything. Now it's solved. That was at  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4046360,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the normal pace of AI progress. You didn't \nhave a billion superintelligent researchers. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4050520,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A year is a huge difference, particularly after \nsuperintelligence. Once this is applied to many  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4055080,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "elements of R\u0026D, you get an industrial \nexplosion with robots and other advanced  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4060800,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "technologies. A couple of years might \nyield decades worth of progress. Again,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4064960,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it’s like the technological lead \nthe U.S. had in the first Gulf War,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4065960,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "when the 20-30 years of technological lead \nproved totally decisive. It really matters. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4071120,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Here’s another reason it really matters. Suppose \nthey steal the weights, suppose they steal the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4076600,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "algorithms, and they're close on our tails. \nSuppose we still pull out ahead. We're a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4081960,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "little bit faster and we're three months ahead.\nThe world in which we're really neck and neck,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4087840,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we only have a three-month lead, is incredibly \ndangerous. We're in this feverish struggle where  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4092840,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if they get ahead, they get to dominate, maybe \nthey get a decisive advantage. They're building  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4099880,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "clusters like crazy. They're willing to throw \nall caution to the wind. We have to keep up. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4104040,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are crazy new WMDs popping up. Then we're \ngoing to be in the situation where it's crazy new  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4109040,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "military technology, crazy new WMDs, deterrence, \nmutually assured destruction keeps changing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4114520,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "every few weeks. It's a completely unstable, \nvolatile situation that is incredibly dangerous. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4119760,
    "dDurationMs": 2720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So you have to look at it from the point of \nview that these technologies are dangerous,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4122480,
    "dDurationMs": 3320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from the alignment point of view. It might be \nreally important during the intelligence explosion  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4125800,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to have a six-month wiggle room to be like, “look, \nwe're going to dedicate more compute to alignment  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4131640,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "during this period because we have to get it \nright. We're feeling uneasy about how it's going.” "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4137880,
    "dDurationMs": 3640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One of the most important inputs to \nwhether we will destroy ourselves or  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4141520,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "whether we will get through this incredibly \ncrazy period is whether we have that buffer. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4149480,
    "dDurationMs": 9920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Before we go further, it's very much worth noting \nthat almost nobody I talk to thinks about the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4159400,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "geopolitical implications of AI. I have some \nobject-level disagreements that we'll get into,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4166320,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "things I want to iron out. I \nmay not disagree in the end. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4170560,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The basic premise is that if you keep scaling, if \npeople realize that this is where intelligence is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4177160,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "headed, it's not just going to be the same old \nworld. It won't just be about what model we're  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4182920,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "deploying tomorrow or what the latest thing \nis. People on Twitter are like, \"oh, GPT-4 is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4188760,
    "dDurationMs": 7800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "going to shake your expectations\" or whatever.\nCOVID is really interesting because when March  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4196560,
    "dDurationMs": 7880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "2020 hit, it became clear to the world \n— presidents, CEOs, media, the average  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4204440,
    "dDurationMs": 3400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "person — that there are other things happening \nin the world right now but the main thing we  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4207840,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as a world are dealing with right now is COVID.\nSoon it will be AGI. This is the quiet period.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4214680,
    "dDurationMs": 9520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Maybe you want to go on vacation. Maybe now is the \nlast time you can have some kids. My girlfriend  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4224200,
    "dDurationMs": 8280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sometimes complains when I’m off doing work that \nI don’t spend enough time with her. She threatens  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4232480,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to replace me with GPT-6 or whatever. I'm like, \n“GPT-6 will also be too busy doing AI research.” "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4243600,
    "dDurationMs": 1680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Why aren't other people talking \nabout national security? "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4245280,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I made this mistake with COVID. In February \nof 2020, I thought it was going to sweep  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4251320,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the world and all the hospitals would \ncollapse. It would be crazy, and then  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4255520,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it'd be over. A lot of people thought this kind \nof thing at the beginning of COVID. They shut  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4258960,
    "dDurationMs": 3320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "down their office for a month or whatever.\nThe thing I just really didn't price in was  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4262280,
    "dDurationMs": 7720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "societal reaction. Within weeks, Congress \nspent over 10% of GDP on COVID measures.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4270000,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The entire country was shut down. It was crazy. \nI didn't sufficiently price it in with COVID. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4278800,
    "dDurationMs": 8880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Why do people underrate it? Being in the \ntrenches actually gives you a less clear  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4287680,
    "dDurationMs": 3080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "picture of the trend lines. You don’t have \nto zoom out that much, only a few years. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4292520,
    "dDurationMs": 3160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When you're in the trenches, you're trying \nto get the next model to work. There's always  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4295680,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "something that's hard. You might underrate \nalgorithmic progress because you're like,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4299520,
    "dDurationMs": 2200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "\"ah, things are hard right now,\" or \"data wall\""
    } ]
  } ]
}
